                            <comment id="17956597" author="sergeychugunov" created="Fri, 6 Jun 2025 13:53:13 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;My findings when fixing this test.&lt;br/&gt;
Test name itself suggests that it is about proper handling of discard messages that are responsible for removing pending messages from internal queue in ServerImpl. But the test implementation is rather vague and doesn&apos;t make it obvious what is checked and how these checks are done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It looks more like a smoke test verifying that pending messages are not resent after being discarded in situation when a node in cluster is restarted. I examined an unmodified version of the test and found out that when it fails locally, there are always some not discarded pending messages. It means that there is a race within the test. But at the same time closing the race makes the test look weird, like it verifies nothing. Well, if we allow pending messages to be discarded (basically what we need to do to close the race), we don&apos;t expect these messages to be resent again. So why to verify later that are not resent? That&apos;s confusing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One possible explanation for the existence of the test is that at some point in time pending messages functionality wasn&apos;t stable enough, and already discarded messages could indeed be resent after another node restart. Anyway, I see this test as a useful one as pending messages functionality is pretty complicated, and even smoke tests don&apos;t hurt, especially a lightweight ones.&lt;/p&gt;
</comment>
